A||||1||BN Boomer||Swapping an 88 2.8 V6 into an 85 GT||||1||
Z||000000||BN Boomer||01-02-2001||06:29 PM||||I'm going to be swapping an 88 2.8 V6 into my 85 GT. The donor car is a 5 speed and my GT is an automatic. If I remember correctly, the 88 V6 was balanced differently than the previous years and therefore has a unique flex plate/flywheel. Is this correct? Are the flex plates used on later 3.1 and 3.4 60 degree V6 engines acceptable subsitutes if I am unable to find an original flex plate from an 88 V6 Fiero? <P>Does anyone have a flex plate they are willing to part with or know of source of reasonably priced ones?<P>Thanks, Boomer||||reg||1
Z||000001||Standard||01-03-2001||02:52 AM||||88's are balanced internally, because the weight of the DIS reference disc on the crankshaft eliminated the need for counterweights on the flywheel or flexplate.  The crank was used starting in 88 for rear-wheel-drive cars, even though they still used distributors.<P>I know you can use the flywheels from 3.1's and 3.4's for a manual 2.8, so it would make sense that you can also use a flexplate..||||reg||1
